## Problem Statement

The customer requested a dedicated and secure SFTP location for exchanging files with the production environment.

Requirements included:

* Create a dedicated SFTP directory.
* Provision a new Linux user.
* Generate a secure password following Linux password standards.
* Configure appropriate ownership and permissions.
* Validate file upload and download functionality.

---

## Requirements

* Create a dedicated SFTP user.
* Create the requested directory structure.
* Apply secure ownership and permissions.
* Ensure only the intended user has access.
* Validate end-to-end SFTP functionality.
* Share credentials securely with the customer.

---

## Implementation

### Step 1 – Create the Directory Structure

Created the requested SFTP directory.

```bash
mkdir -p /app2/MERIDA
```

Verified the directory was created successfully.

---

### Step 2 – Create the Linux User

Provisioned a dedicated SFTP user.

```bash
useradd merida
```

Assigned a strong password compliant with Linux security standards.

Password characteristics included:

* Uppercase letters
* Lowercase letters
* Numbers
* Special characters
* Minimum security length

The password was shared securely with the customer through the approved communication channel.

---

### Step 3 – Configure Directory Ownership

Assigned ownership of the directory.

Example:

```bash
chown merida:merida /app2/MERIDA
```

Applied appropriate directory permissions.

```bash
chmod 750 /app2/MERIDA
```

This ensured only authorized users could access the directory while protecting the contents from unauthorized access.

---

### Step 4 – Validate SFTP Access

Verified user authentication.

Connected using SFTP.

```bash
sftp merida@10.230.200.55
```

Successfully authenticated.

---

### Step 5 – Functional Validation

Performed end-to-end testing.

Uploaded a test file.

```text
put testfile.txt
```

Downloaded the same file.

```text
get testfile.txt
```

Validated:

* Successful login
* File upload
* File download
* File integrity
* Directory permissions

No permission or authentication errors were observed.

